<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fourteen Points</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fourteen Points were a statement of principles for peace issued by US President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> in 1918. They called for self-determination, including autonomous development for the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, encouraging national movements that contributed to the break-up of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourteen-points.jpg" alt="Fourteen Points" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Orpen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Orpen_Woodrow_Wilson_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on 6 April 1917, in part because of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s resumption of submarine warfare and the Zimmermann telegram. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> wanted to separate American participation from the nationalist disputes of the European powers. The need for moral aims grew when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> disclosed secret treaties made between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and Wilson was also responding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>'s Decree on Peace of November 1917. Three days before his speech, the British prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a> had set out British war aims at Caxton Hall, and Wilson briefly considered abandoning his own, but his adviser Edward M. House persuaded him to go ahead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Wilson delivered the Fourteen Points in a speech on war aims and peace terms to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> on 8 January 1918. They rested on the research of the Inquiry, a team of about 150 advisers led by House.</p><p>The first four points called for open covenants of peace openly arrived at, absolute freedom of navigation on the seas, the removal of economic barriers, and guarantees that national armaments would be reduced to the lowest point consistent with domestic safety. Points ten to thirteen dealt with colonial disputes and the autonomous development of peoples, and point thirteen called for a reborn Polish state with free and secure access to the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The speech was spread as Allied propaganda, translated into many languages, and dropped behind German lines. In October 1918 the German chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Max of Baden</a> asked Wilson for an armistice and peace negotiations on the basis of the Fourteen Points. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> they became a symbol of the promised basis of peace, and through the interwar period critics argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> contradicted them. The Allied leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a> and Vittorio Emanuele Orlando were sceptical of Wilsonian idealism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> called the document high-sounding and meaningless.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> peace conference the points guided disputes over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and Danzig, which the treaty made a Free City with special Polish rights. They also drew the attention of anti-colonial leaders, who saw an opportunity to seek independence. The Korean March First Movement of 1919 and the Egyptian Revolution of 1919 followed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> did not support either.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fourteen Points” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>President Wilson's <b>Fourteen Points</b> of January 1918 called for the autonomous development of the peoples of Austria-Hungary, and the Allies' subsequent recognition of the Czechoslovak National Council encouraged the independence movements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century its power shaped the world order: Woodrow Wilson's <b>Fourteen Points</b> of 1918 promoted national self-determination, and after 1945 the United States led the creation of the United Nations, the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank, and financed European recovery through the Marshall Plan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After Versailles, Western liberal democracy lost much of its appeal among Chinese intellectuals, who saw Wilson’s <b>Fourteen Points</b> as hypocritical and noted that the United States had declined to join the League of Nations; with the October Revolution, Marxism took hold.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
